<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Adapted from FOPP "Lists and for loops", "Strings and for loops", and
     "Traversal and the for Loop: By Index". GNU FDL 1.3; see for-loops/ch-loops.ptx. -->
<section xml:id="sec-arrays-by-index">
  <title>Looping Over an Array</title>
  <introduction>

    <p>
      Here is where loops start to pay off.
      Remember that every element in an array has an index, starting at 0.
    </p>

    <program language="javascript">
      <code>
  let friends = ["Joe", "Amy", "Brad", "Zuki"];
  //   index:      0      1      2       3
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      The indexes of this array are 0, 1, 2, 3.
      That is exactly the list of values <c>i</c> takes in <c>for (let i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++)</c>.
      So if we use <c>i</c> inside the brackets, each pass of the loop gets the next element.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="try-arr-1">
      <title>Party Invitations</title>
      <program xml:id="ac-arr-1" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
      <code>
  let friends = ["Joe", "Amy", "Brad", "Zuki"];

  for (let i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {
    console.log("Hi " + friends[i] + ", please come to my party on Saturday!");
  }
      </code>
    </program>
    </activity>

    <p>
      On the first pass, <c>i</c> is 0, so <c>friends[i]</c> is <c>friends[0]</c>, which is <c>"Joe"</c>.
      On the second pass, <c>i</c> is 1, so <c>friends[i]</c> is <c>"Amy"</c>.
      And so on, until <c>i</c> is 4 and the loop stops.
    </p>

    <p>
      Visiting every element of an array, one at a time, is called <term>traversing</term> the array,
      or <term>iterating over</term> the array.
    </p>

    <note>
      <title>The test's definition</title>
      <p>
        Operation Spark's test defines iteration as:
        <q>Iteration is the process of accessing values in an Array using a loop.</q>
        Learn those words for the test.
        As this chapter shows, iteration really means repeating any code.
        Going through an array is the most common reason to do it, but not the only one.
      </p>
    </note>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-use-length">
    <title>Use <c>.length</c>, Not a Number</title>

    <p>
      The loop above has a problem. It only works if the array has exactly 4 elements.
      Add a friend and they will not get invited.
      Instead of writing the number, use the array's <c>.length</c>:
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="try-arr-2">
      <title>Invitations with <c>.length</c></title>
      <program xml:id="ac-arr-2" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
      <code>
let friends = ["Joe", "Amy", "Brad", "Zuki", "Thandi", "Paris"];

for (let i = 0; i &lt; friends.length; i++) {
  console.log("Hi " + friends[i] + ", please come to my party on Saturday!");
}
      </code>
    </program>
    </activity>

    <p>
      This is the most common loop in JavaScript.
      Learn it until you can write it without thinking:
    </p>

    <program language="javascript">
      <code>
for (let i = 0; i &lt; array.length; i++) {
  // array[i] is the current element
}
      </code>
    </program>

    <p>
      Notice the condition is <c>&lt;</c>, not <c>&lt;=</c>.
      An array with 6 elements has indexes 0 through 5. There is no index 6.
      If you use <c>&lt;=</c>, the last pass asks for <c>friends[6]</c>, which does not exist,
      and you get <c>undefined</c>. Try it: change <c>&lt;</c> to <c>&lt;=</c> above and run it.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-index-mistake">
    <title>Use <c>i</c> Inside the Brackets</title>

    <p>
      A common mistake is to write a number inside the brackets instead of the loop variable.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="try-arr-3">
      <title>The Same Name Every Time</title>
      <program xml:id="ac-arr-3" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
      <code>
let friends = ["Joe", "Amy", "Brad", "Zuki"];

for (let i = 0; i &lt; friends.length; i++) {
  console.log(friends[0]);
}
      </code>
    </program>
    </activity>

    <p>
      This loop runs 4 times, but it prints <c>Joe</c> every time,
      because <c>friends[0]</c> always means the first element.
      The loop variable is the only thing that changes from pass to pass,
      so the loop variable is what has to go in the brackets.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-strings-by-index">
    <title>Strings Work the Same Way</title>

    <p>
      A string also has a <c>.length</c>, and you can get one character with bracket notation.
      So the same loop works on a string, one character at a time.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="try-arr-4">
      <title>Letters of a Word</title>
      <program xml:id="ac-arr-4" interactive="activecode" language="javascript">
      <code>
let word = "banana";

for (let i = 0; i &lt; word.length; i++) {
  console.log(i + ": " + word[i]);
}
      </code>
    </program>
    </activity>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="sec-arrays-by-index-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-arr-every-index" label="mc-arr-every-index">
      <statement>
        <p>A loop starts with <c>let i = 0</c> and needs to visit every index of an array named <c>scores</c>, no matter how many elements it has. Which condition should it use?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>i &lt; scores.length</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. It starts at the first index, 0, and stops after the last index, <c>scores.length - 1</c>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>i &lt;= scores.length</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. On the last pass, <c>i</c> equals <c>scores.length</c>, which is one past the last index.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>i &lt; scores.length - 1</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. This stops one index early and skips the last element.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>i &lt; 10</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. This only works if <c>scores</c> has exactly 10 elements.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-arr-print-each" label="mc-arr-print-each">
      <statement>
        <p>What does this program print?</p>
        <program language="javascript">
          <code>
let clubs = ["Robotics", "Chess", "Drama", "Art"];

for (let i = 0; i &lt; clubs.length; i++) {
  console.log(clubs[0]);
}
          </code>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>Robotics</c>, four times</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. <c>clubs[0]</c> is always the first element. The loop variable is not in the brackets, so nothing changes from pass to pass.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Each club once: <c>Robotics</c>, <c>Chess</c>, <c>Drama</c>, <c>Art</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. That would need <c>clubs[i]</c>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>0</c>, <c>1</c>, <c>2</c>, <c>3</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. The program prints an element of the array, not the index.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>Robotics</c>, once</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. The loop runs once per element, and the <c>console.log</c> is inside it.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="mc-arr-undefined" label="mc-arr-undefined">
      <statement>
        <p>What does the last pass of this loop print?</p>
        <program language="javascript">
          <code>
  let colors = ["red", "green", "blue"];

  for (let i = 0; i &lt;= colors.length; i++) {
    console.log(colors[i]);
  }
          </code>
        </program>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>undefined</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Right. The last pass has <c>i</c> equal to 3, and there is no <c>colors[3]</c>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>blue</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. <c>blue</c> is printed, but it is not the last thing printed.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>red</c></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>No. The loop does not go back to the start of the array.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>
</section>
